  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 336 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nActive weather returns on Monday evening bringing increased moisture \nand precipitation chances for much of the area. A frontal boundary \nwill push through Nebraska on Monday resulting in some rain and \nthunderstorm chances by the evening. As this time, the severe threat \nremains marginal which correlates well to the latest SPC guidance. \nCurrent thinking is that the majority of thunderstorm activity will \nremain across South Dakota as convection will struggle to develop \nfurther to the south into Nebraska where a more prominent cap will \nremain in place. If thunderstorms can develop, the environment will \nremain favorable for some stronger to briefly severe thunderstorms. \nOtherwise, expecting these storms to remain fairly isolated \nespecially to the south and west where less instability is expected. \n\nAnother round of thunderstorms return on Tuesday evening with \nanother potential for some severe storms. While the severe risk \nremains low at this time, would not be surprised to see some \nisolated severe storms again with large hail and damaging winds as \nthe main threats. \n\nUpper level ridging and surface high pressure will build back into \nthe region mid-week and outside of a low potential for some isolated \nshowers Wednesday morning, conditions will remain dry through the \nend of the week and into early next weekend. This pattern shift will \nalso allow for a gradual warming trend into next weekend. Highs will \nrise from the 70s on Tuesday back into the 90s on Saturday. \nOvernight lows will also drop from the mid 60s to low 70s Monday \nmorning to the 50s by Tuesday morning. They will also slowly \nincrease back into the 60s by Friday morning.",
